  "Number": 3,
  "Question": "Isaac has written down one integer two times and another integer three times. The sum of the five numbers is \\(100,\\) and one of the numbers is \\(28.\\) What is the other number?",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(8\\)",
    "B": "\\(11\\)",
    "C": "\\(14\\)",
    "D": "\\(15\\)",
    "E": "\\(18\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "A",
  "Solution": "The number \\(28\\) must occur either twice or three times, so there are only two cases to check.\n\nIf it occurs twice, those copies contribute \\(2\\cdot28=56\\). The remaining sum is \\(100-56=44\\), which would have to be three times the other integer. This is impossible because \\(44\\) is not divisible by \\(3\\).\n\nTherefore \\(28\\) occurs three times. The two copies of the other number have sum\n\\[\n100-3\\cdot28=16,\n\\]\nso the other number is \\(16\\div2=8\\). This works because \\(3\\cdot28+2\\cdot8=100\\). The answer is \\(\\boxed{8}\\), choice A.",
